WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.389
Running local inference begins with loading a compatible GGUF model and sending

00:00:04.389 --> 00:00:06.175
a prompt to the runtime.

00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:08.320
At first, keep the configuration simple.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.251
Use a known model, a short prompt, a reasonable output length,

00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:13.680
and conservative context settings.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.326
The first goal is to prove that the model loads, the tokenizer works,

00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:22.615
and the runtime produces output through the path you intend to use.

00:00:22.615 --> 00:00:25.474
After the basic path works, tune operational settings.

00:00:25.474 --> 00:00:29.048
Context length controls how much input the model can consider.

00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:31.192
Thread count can affect CPU performance.

00:00:31.192 --> 00:00:35.481
GPU offload can move supported model computation to the GPU when hardware

00:00:35.481 --> 00:00:37.268
and runtime configuration allow it.

00:00:37.268 --> 00:00:40.127
Output length controls how much text is generated.

00:00:40.127 --> 00:00:42.271
Sampling settings influence determinism and variation.

00:00:42.271 --> 00:00:45.487
Observe prompt processing speed and token generation speed separately.

00:00:45.487 --> 00:00:48.704
A long prompt may take time before output begins.

00:00:48.704 --> 00:00:51.920
A large or poorly matched model may generate slowly.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.566
If the system swaps memory or runs out of VRAM, performance may collapse.

00:00:56.566 --> 00:01:00.855
A small benchmark based on your actual workload is more useful than

00:01:00.855 --> 00:01:03.714
a generic performance claim from a different machine.

00:01:03.714 --> 00:01:07.645
Save working command examples as documented configurations, not hidden one-off experiments.

00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:11.933
Include model name, file path, context length, thread settings, GPU offload choices,

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:14.435
sampling settings, runtime version, and hardware notes.

00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:18.009
That documentation lets you reproduce a good setup, compare changes,

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:21.940
and recover when an upgrade or model swap causes unexpected behavior.
